  "content": "# Implementing a Bounded LRU Cache in Python\n\n## Overview\n\nA **Least Recently Used (LRU) cache** is a memory management policy that discards the least recently used items when the cache reaches its capacity. This is widely used in databases, HTTP caches, and operating systems. Python\u2019s `collections.OrderedDict` provides an ideal foundation because it maintains insertion order and allows efficient removal of the first item.\n\nBelow is a complete, production-ready implementation with detailed explanations, edge cases, tests, a worked example, and complexity analysis.\n\n---\n\n## Complete Implementation\n\n```python\nfrom collections import OrderedDict\nfrom typing import Hashable, Any\n\n\nclass LRUCache:\n    \"\"\"\n    A Least Recently Used cache with a bounded size.\n    \n    The cache evicts the least recently used key when it reaches capacity.\n    Uses OrderedDict to maintain access order efficiently.\n    \n    Attributes:\n        capacity (int): Maximum number of items the cache can hold.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, capacity: int):\n        \"\"\"\n        Initialize an LRU cache with the given capacity.\n        \n        Args:\n            capacity: Maximum number of items in the cache. Must be positive.\n        \n        Raises:\n            ValueError: If capacity is less than 1.\n        \"\"\"\n        if capacity < 1:\n            raise ValueError(\"Capacity must be at least 1.\")\n        self.capacity = capacity\n        self.cache = OrderedDict()  # key -> value, ordered by most-recent access\n\n    def get(self, key: Hashable) -> Any:\n        \"\"\"\n        Retrieve a value from the cache.\n        \n        If the key exists, its access time is updated (moved to the end\n        of the OrderedDict, making it the most recently used).\n        \n        Args:\n            key: The key to look up.\n        \n        Returns:\n            The value associated with the key, or -1 if not found.\n        \"\"\"\n        if key not in self.cache:\n            return -1  # Default \"not found\" sentinel\n        \n        # Move the key to the end (most recently used position)\n        self.cache.move_to_end(key)\n        return self.cache[key]\n\n    def put(self, key: Hashable, value: Any) -> None:\n        \"\"\"\n        Insert or update a key-value pair in the cache.\n        \n        If the key already exists, its",
